WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.164
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:08.990
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:16.122
Unit four: Organizational Design. Today you will discuss structural choices with balance and trade-off language.

4
00:00:16.422 --> 00:00:19.132
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.230
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:25.259
Org structure.

7
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.421
Org structure means the formal arrangement of roles, teams and authority.

8
00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:33.980
The new org structure removed two layers of management.

9
00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:37.352
Your turn. Say: Org structure.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.584
Org structure.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.328
Hierarchy.

12
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:47.535
Hierarchy means a system of ranks from top to bottom.

13
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.160
A steep hierarchy can slow decision-making.

14
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.553
Your turn. Say: Hierarchy.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.645
Hierarchy.

16
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:00.791
Span of control.

17
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:06.093
Span of control means the number of employees a manager directly supervises.

18
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:10.297
A span of control of thirty is too wide for one supervisor.

19
00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:13.826
Your turn. Say: Span of control.

20
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:18.202
Span of control.

21
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.566
Matrix organization.

22
00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:25.790
Matrix organization means a structure in which staff report to two managers.

23
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:31.669
In a matrix organization, engineers report to both product and region heads.

24
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:35.377
Your turn. Say: Matrix organization.

25
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:40.105
Matrix organization.

26
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.211
Decentralization.

27
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:48.351
Decentralization means giving decision-making power to lower levels or local units.

28
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.320
Decentralization let provincial branches respond faster.

29
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:55.743
Your turn. Say: Decentralization.

30
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.089
Decentralization.

31
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.072
Role clarity.

32
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:06.952
Role clarity means a clear understanding of one's responsibilities.

33
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.316
Lack of role clarity caused duplicated work.

34
00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:13.766
Your turn. Say: Role clarity.

35
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.752
Role clarity.

36
00:02:18.517 --> 00:02:19.704
Reporting line.

37
00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:24.272
Reporting line means the formal link showing who reports to whom.

38
00:02:24.883 --> 00:02:27.498
Her reporting line goes directly to the CEO.

39
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:31.084
Your turn. Say: Reporting line.

40
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:35.178
Reporting line.

41
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:36.918
Agility.

42
00:02:37.917 --> 00:02:41.402
Agility means the ability to adapt quickly to change.

43
00:02:42.017 --> 00:02:44.550
Small teams give the company greater agility.

44
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.884
Your turn. Say: Agility.

45
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:51.839
Agility.

46
00:02:52.633 --> 00:02:56.458
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.317 --> 00:03:04.253
Visal, the microfinance firm you studied moved decisions from Phnom Penh to its provincial branches. Was that wise?

48
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:16.489
Largely, yes. Loans are approved in two days instead of two weeks, but the firm must balance that speed with consistent risk control.

49
00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:19.989
Didn't some branches start lending too freely?

50
00:03:21.083 --> 00:03:30.574
A few did. That's the classic trade-off: decentralization increases agility at the cost of central oversight.

51
00:03:31.683 --> 00:03:33.648
So how did they strike a balance?

52
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:45.169
They set clear lending limits and shared data dashboards. Branches have freedom within boundaries, which offsets the loss of control.

53
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:47.764
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:51.557
Grammar focus: Balance and tradeoff language.

55
00:03:51.857 --> 00:03:53.136
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.011
Organizations must balance structure with agility.

57
00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:01.646
Decentralization increases speed at the cost of central control.

58
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.616
Managers need to strike a balance between autonomy and consistency.

59
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:18.857
Useful trade-off phrases: balance X with Y, strike a balance between X and Y, X comes at the cost of Y, X is offset by Y. Avoid 'balance between X with Y'.

60
00:04:19.617 --> 00:04:24.254
Be careful! Don't say: We must balance between speed with control.

61
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.186
Say: We must balance speed with control.

62
00:04:29.486 --> 00:04:31.045
Balance X with Y.

63
00:04:31.833 --> 00:04:34.439
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:37.279
Designing for Speed and Control.

65
00:04:37.579 --> 00:04:43.155
Organizational design determines how authority, information and responsibility flow through a company.

66
00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:50.799
Traditional hierarchies offer clear reporting lines, but they can slow decisions as information travels up and down many levels.

67
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:58.432
Flatter structures widen each manager's span of control, gaining speed at the cost of closer supervision.

68
00:04:59.433 --> 00:05:07.981
Matrix organizations attempt to strike a balance between functional expertise and regional responsiveness, although dual reporting can create confusion.

69
00:05:08.967 --> 00:05:15.761
No structure is perfect, so leaders must continually balance stability with the agility that fast-changing markets demand.

70
00:05:16.867 --> 00:05:21.252
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:22.117 --> 00:05:24.778
Leaders must balance cost blank quality.

72
00:05:25.078 --> 00:05:28.116
A, with. B, from. C, to.

73
00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:33.376
The answer is A: with.

74
00:05:34.517 --> 00:05:37.608
The number of employees a manager supervises is the blank.

75
00:05:37.908 --> 00:05:42.606
A, reporting line. B, span of control. C, hierarchy.

76
00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:48.723
The answer is B: span of control.

77
00:05:49.883 --> 00:05:53.067
Giving decision-making power to local units is blank.

78
00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:58.298
A, decentralization. B, role clarity. C, a matrix organization.

79
00:06:01.833 --> 00:06:04.599
The answer is A: decentralization.

80
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.610
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:16.068
A, Speed came at the cost of accuracy.. B, Speed came at the cost for accuracy.. C, Speed came on the cost of accuracy..

82
00:06:19.633 --> 00:06:23.328
The answer is A: Speed came at the cost of accuracy..

83
00:06:24.483 --> 00:06:28.282
According to the reading, a weakness of matrix organizations is that…

84
00:06:28.582 --> 00:06:34.430
A, they are too small. B, dual reporting can create confusion. C, they have no managers.

85
00:06:37.967 --> 00:06:41.626
The answer is B: dual reporting can create confusion.

86
00:06:42.833 --> 00:06:45.880
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:46.717 --> 00:06:50.678
What are the tradeoffs between a centralized and decentralized organization?

88
00:07:07.483 --> 00:07:08.984
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:09.284 --> 00:07:47.052
A centralized organization offers consistency and tight control, because important decisions are made by a small group at the top. However, this often comes at the cost of speed, since local managers must wait for approval. A decentralized organization, by contrast, allows branches to respond quickly to customers, but it risks inconsistent standards. A bank with many provincial branches must balance local flexibility with national risk rules. I think the best design depends on how fast the market changes.

90
00:07:47.352 --> 00:07:49.201
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:50.117 --> 00:07:54.650
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and balance and tradeoff language.

92
00:07:54.950 --> 00:08:01.120
Today's value is organizational clarity. Make sure everyone knows their role and responsibilities.

93
00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.887
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

94
00:08:04.187 --> 00:08:06.874
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

